    id: "types-2-adts",
    label: "Types 2: define-type, match & exhaustiveness",
    code: ";; define-type declares a CLOSED datatype: the compiler knows every\n;; constructor, so it checks exhaustiveness, field types, and arity.\n;; It is a grammar plus constructors (docs/GRAMMARS.md): instances are\n;; plain s-expressions -- (Circle 3) is the list (Circle 3), Point the\n;; symbol Point -- so quoted data and constructors are the same values.\n\n(define-type Shape\n  (Point)\n  (Circle Int)\n  (Rect Int Int))\n\n(define (area [s : Shape]) : Int\n  (match s\n    [Point 0]\n    [(Circle r) (* 3 (* r r))]\n    [(Rect w h) (* w h)]))\n\n(println (area (Circle 3)))       ;; 27\n(println (area '(Rect 4 5)))      ;; 20 -- a quote is checked against Shape too\n\n(println (Circle 3))              ;; (Circle 3)\n(println Point)                   ;; Point -- nullary ctors are bare symbols\n(println (vector? (Circle 3)))    ;; #f\n(println (equal? (Circle 3) '(Circle 3)))  ;; #t -- one world of data\n\n;; ---- uncomment: a non-exhaustive match is a WARNING (it still\n;; runs; `puffincc --strict-types` promotes it to an error) ----\n\n;; (define (radius [s : Shape]) : Int\n;;   (match s [(Circle r) r]))\n;; (println (radius (Circle 9)))\n;;   typecheck warning: match on Shape is not exhaustive: missing Point, Rect [main.puf:29]\n;;   9\n\n;; ---- uncomment for compile-time errors ----\n\n;; (Circle \"big\")\n;;   error: typecheck: Circle: argument has type Str, expected Int [main.puf:37]\n\n;; (Circle 1 2)\n;;   error: typecheck: Circle expects 1 arguments, got 2 [main.puf:40]\n\n;; (area '(Rect 4))\n;;   error: typecheck: area: argument has type '(Rect 4), expected Shape [main.puf:43]\n",
