    id: "types-3-casts",
    label: "Types 3: casts & blame (whole-term grammar casts)",
    code: ";; The checker can't see through _ -- that's what makes it gradual.\n;; Instead, every DECLARED boundary is guarded by a transient cast:\n;; a lie that crosses an unannotated seam dies AT the boundary, at\n;; runtime, with a blame label naming it.\n\n(define (dist2 [x : Int] [y : Int]) : Int\n  (+ (* x x) (* y y)))\n\n;; an untyped caller: p is _, and _ ~ Int checks fine at compile time\n(define (untyped-caller p)\n  (dist2 (car p) (cdr p)))\n(println (untyped-caller (cons 3 4)))     ;; 25\n\n;; grammar and define-type casts check the WHOLE term, not just its\n;; outermost shape (docs/GRAMMARS.md)\n(define-type (Option a) (None) (Some a))\n(define (unwrap [o : (Option Int)]) : Int\n  (match o [(Some x) x] [None 0]))\n(println (unwrap (Some 5)))               ;; 5\n\n(define-grammar Arith Int (+ Arith Arith))\n(define (sum [e : Arith]) : Int\n  (match e [(? fixnum? n) n] [`(+ ,a ,b) (+ (sum a) (sum b))]))\n(define (untyped-sum e) (sum e))          ;; e is _: the boundary is sum's\n(println (untyped-sum '(+ 1 (+ 2 3))))    ;; 6\n\n;; ---- uncomment: the lie compiles (it hides behind _) but the cast\n;; fires at the boundary, with blame ----\n\n;; (println (untyped-caller (cons #t 4)))\n;;   puffin runtime error: cast: expected Int, got #t (blame: dist2's argument x [main.puf:6])\n\n;; (println ((ann unwrap _) 42))\n;;   puffin runtime error: cast: expected (Option Int), got 42 (blame: unwrap's argument o [main.puf:17])\n\n;; (println (untyped-sum '(+ 1 (+ 2 \"three\"))))\n;;   puffin runtime error: cast: expected Arith, got (+ 1 (+ 2 three)) (blame: sum's argument e [main.puf:22])\n\n;; note the difference: without the _ disguise the same mistakes are\n;; caught at COMPILE time -- try (unwrap 42), (dist2 #t 4), or\n;; (sum '(+ 1 (+ 2 \"three\"))).\n;; Fully unannotated programs carry no casts at all -- they compile\n;; byte-identically to how they did before types existed.\n",
